he billiard ball is reflected twice and starts to move the opposite way. While it moves, the billiard ball can make an infinite number of reflections. At any square of its trajectory the billiard ball can stop and on that the move is considered completed.\n \nIt is considered that one billiard ball a beats another billiard ball b if a can reach a point where b is located.\n\nYou are suggested to find the maximal number of billiard balls, that pairwise do not beat each other and that can be positioned on a chessboard n ΓÇë├ùΓÇë m in size.\n\n## Input\n\nThe first line contains two integers n and m ( 2ΓÇëΓëñΓÇë n ,ΓÇë m ΓÇëΓëñΓÇë10 6 ).\n\n## Output\n\nPrint a single number, the maximum possible number of billiard balls that do not pairwise beat each other.\n\nPlease do not use the %lld specificator to read or write 64-bit numbers in C++. It is preferred to use cin (also you may use the %I64d specificator).\n\n## Examples\n\nExample 1:\n```\n3 4\n```\n```\n2\n```","type":"text"}],"contestId":74,"index":"C","memoryLimit":"256 megabytes","name":"Chessboard Billiard","problemKey":"74-C","rating":null,"tags":[],"timeLimit":"2 seconds","url":"https://codeforces.com/contest/74/problem/C"},{"approaches":[{"label":"Approach 1","number":1,"solutions":[{"code":"","fileName":"main.cpp","language":"cpp"}]},{"label":"Approach 2","number":2,"solutions":[{"code":"","fileName":"main.cpp","language":"cpp"}]},{"label":"Approach 3","number":3,"solutions":[{"code":"","fileName":"main.cpp","language":"cpp"}]},{"label":"Approach 4","number":4,"solutions":[{"code":"","fileName":"main.cpp","language":"cpp"}]},{"label":"Approach 5","number":5,"solutions":[{"code":"","fileName":"main.cpp","language":"cpp"}]},{"label":"Approach 6","number":6,"solutions":[{"code":"","fileName":"main.cpp","language":"cpp"}]},{"label":"Approach 7","number":7,"solutions":[{"code":"","fileName":"main.cpp","language":"cpp"}]},{"label":"Approach 8","number":8,"solutions":[{"code":"","fileName":"main.cpp","language":"c